<section id="concurrency" class="chapter"><div class="chapter-title"><span class="index">10</span><div><h2>Choose the concurrency you actually need</h2><p class="deck">Ownership continues across threads and tasks.</p></div></div>
<p><code>Send</code> indicates that a value's ownership can cross thread boundaries. <code>Sync</code> means shared references can cross them safely. Arc provides atomic shared ownership, not automatic thread safety for arbitrary contents. Mutex provides synchronized access; short lock scopes make behavior easier to reason about. Scoped threads can borrow data because the scope waits for its threads.</p>
<Example name="thread" />
<h3>Async is a scheduling contract</h3><p>An async function produces a future. A runtime drives it; await can suspend the current task. A ready future need not yield, so merely writing await in a loop does not establish fairness. Async helps with concurrent waiting; it does not automatically make CPU work faster.</p>
<article class="wisdom"><p class="eyebrow">Practitioner note / Alice Ryhl</p><h3>Do not occupy an executor thread with blocking work.</h3><p>Ryhl explains why synchronous sleeps or expensive computation can stall other tasks in a cooperative executor. Current Tokio documentation supports moving bounded blocking work into spawn_blocking, limiting concurrent CPU jobs, and using dedicated threads for persistent blocking loops. Once a spawn_blocking closure starts, abort will not stop it.</p><p class="application">Apply it: decide timeouts, cancellation behavior, queue limits and shutdown before spawning a task per input.</p></article>
<p>Start with synchronous code for a small CLI like this one. Add an async runtime when the workload needs concurrent I/O. No runtime dependency is required for the included lessons.</p>
